{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-353068",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=5$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/e83c5729ef5db920a0b451a8571ad84f45d2b4e950ab831ced0da2c0a12cea50.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={y}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,5-({y}^{2}+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(16y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
